    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b06",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      187.17,
      282.83,
      202.27
    ],
    "reading_order": 6,
    "text": "Also,\nSQ = y2, ST = PR = y1.\nSo, QT = y2 – y1.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b07",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      203.22,
      288.0,
      217.53
    ],
    "reading_order": 7,
    "text": "Now, applying the Pythagoras theorem in  PTQ, we get",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b08",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      142.8,
      221.73,
      222.08,
      233.99
    ],
    "reading_order": 8,
    "text": "PQ2 = PT2 + QT2",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b09",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      336.47,
      231.15,
      373.2,
      243.82
    ],
    "reading_order": 9,
    "text": "Fig. 7.5",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b10",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      164.64,
      239.01,
      268.4,
      253.87
    ],
    "reading_order": 10,
    "text": "= (x2 – x1)2 + (y2 – y1)2",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b11",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      258.82,
      281.72,
      277.57
    ],
    "reading_order": 11,
    "text": "Therefore,\nPQ =\n\n\n\n\n2\n2\n2\n1\n2\n1\nx\nx\ny\ny\n\n\n",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b12",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      285.09,
      420.02,
      313.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 12,
    "text": "Note that since distance is always non-negative, we take only the positive square\nroot. So, the distance between the points P(x1, y1) and Q(x2, y2) is",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b13",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      146.16,
      318.1,
      294.11,
      336.85
    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "PQ =\n\n\n\n\n2\n2\n2\n1\n2\n1\n–\n+\n–\nx\nx\ny\ny\n,",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      343.95,
      216.83,
      356.62
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "which is called the distance formula.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      360.99,
      99.85,
      373.66
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "Remarks :",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b16",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      378.69,
      401.29,
      390.94
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "1. In particular, the distance of a point P(x, y) from the origin O(0, 0) is given by",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b17",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      166.32,
      402.77,
      242.27,
      417.47
    ],
    "reading_order": 17,
    "text": "OP = \n2\n2\nx\ny\n\n.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b18",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      424.9,
      327.84,
      443.65
    ],
    "reading_order": 18,
    "text": "2. We can also write, PQ = \n\n\n\n\n2\n2\n1\n2\n1\n2\nx\nx\ny\ny\n\n\n\n. (Why?)",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b19",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      452.92,
      420.01,
      478.79
    ],
    "reading_order": 19,
    "text": "Example 1 : Do the points (3, 2), (–2, –3) and (2, 3) form a triangle? If so, name the\ntype of triangle formed.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b20",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      483.16,
      420.02,
      509.03
    ],
    "reading_order": 20,
    "text": "Solution : Let us apply the distance formula to find the distances PQ, QR and PR,\nwhere P(3, 2), Q(–2, –3) and R(2, 3) are the given points. We have",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b21",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      520.85,
      351.84,
      535.55
    ],
    "reading_order": 21,
    "text": "PQ = \n2\n2\n2\n2\n(3\n2)\n(2\n3)\n5\n5\n50\n\n\n\n\n\n\n = 7.07 (approx.)",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b22",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      546.05,
      391.68,
      560.75
    ],
    "reading_order": 22,
    "text": "QR = \n2\n2\n2\n2\n(–2 – 2)\n(–3 – 3)\n(– 4)\n(–6)\n52\n\n\n\n\n = 7.21 (approx.)",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b23",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      571.25,
      359.99,
      585.95
    ],
    "reading_order": 23,
    "text": "PR = \n2\n2\n2\n2\n(3 – 2)\n(2 – 3)\n1\n( 1)\n2\n\n\n\n\n = 1.41 (approx.)",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH107",
    "page": 4,
    "block_id": "jemh107_p004_b24",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      593.73,
      420.02,
      619.18
    ],
    "reading_order": 24,
    "text": "Since the sum of any two of these distances is greater than the third distance, therefore,\nthe points P, Q and R form a triangle.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
